{"records":[{"id":"vhz7km2w3t","title":"Role of HMGB1/NF-κB/NLRP3 signaling pathway in silicosis combined COPD","description":"Our results revealed GA as HMGB1 inhibitor that could suppress fibrotic progression and alleviate inflammatory in silicosis complicated with COPD through inhibiting fibrosis-related proteins including Vimentin and α-SMA expression in the lung tissue of rat model. HMGB1, NLRP3, NF-κB-p-p65 were activated in silicosis complicated with COPD, which was suppressed by GA treatment in vivo. Furthermore, HMGB1, NLRP3, NF-κB-p-p65 were increased in silicosis complicated with COPD patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-30T08:36:27.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vhz7km2w3t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vhz7km2w3t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mengya","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fm49hhpdg3","title":"Separation-of-function mutants reveal the NF-kB-independent involvement of IkBα in the regulation of intestinal stemness","description":"We have previously shown that the NF-κB inhibitor IkBa binds the chromatin together with PRC2 to confer responsiveness to PRC2 targets in the presence of inflammatory cues. This alternative function has been elusive in both physiological and disease conditions because of the predominant role of IkBa as a negative regulator of NF-κB. Here, we uniquely characterize the specific residues of IkBa that allow the generation of separation-of-function (SOF) mutants that are defective for either NF-κB-related (SOF NF-κB) or chromatin-related (SOF H2A,H4) activities. Expression of IkBa SOF NF-κB, but not SOF H2A/H4, is sufficient to negatively regulate a specific stemness program in intestinal cells, thereby rescuing the differentiation block imposed by IkBa deficiency. By ChIP assay, we demonstrated that IkBa binds to several stemness genes that are transcriptionally repressed upon IkBa SOF NF-κB induction. Our data suggest that SOF mutants provide an exclusive tool for studying IkBa functions in physiology and disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-20T12:20:10.739Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fm49hhpdg3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fm49hhpdg3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Álvarez-Villanueva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Floor","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Solé Font","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luis","last_name":"G Palma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Maqueda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joan","last_name":"Bertran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Teresa","last_name":"Lobo-Jarne","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rajani","last_name":"Rajbhandari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aran","last_name":"Merati","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Marruecos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jordi","last_name":"Villà-Freixa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Iglesias","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Bredel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Bigas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lluis","last_name":"Espinosa","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00ca2c886","name":"Instituto de Salud Carlos III","grant_id":"PI22/00069 "},{"id":"https://ror.org/00ca2c886","name":"Instituto de Salud Carlos III","grant_id":"DTS23/00005"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4r55hvz87r","title":"Negative DNA supercoiling induces genome-wide Cas9 off-target activity. Newton MD et al","description":"CRISPR/Cas9 is a powerful gene-editing technology, however off-target activity remains an important consideration for therapeutic applications. We have previous-ly shown that force stretching DNA induces off-target activity and hypothesised that distortions of the DNA topology in vivo, such as negative DNA supercoiling, could reduce Cas9 specificity. Using single-molecule optical-tweezers, we demonstrate that negative supercoiling λ-DNA induces sequence-specific Cas9 off-target bind-ing at multiple sites, even at low forces. Using an adapted CIRCLE-seq approach, we detect over 10,000 negative supercoiling induced Cas9 off-target double-strand breaks genome wide, caused by increased mismatch tolerance. We further demon-strate in vivo that directed local DNA distortion increases off-target activity in cells and that induced off-target events can be detected during Cas9 genome-editing. These data demonstrate that Cas9 off-target activity is regulated by DNA topology in vitro and in vivo, suggesting that cellular processes, such as transcription and replication, could induce off-target activity at previously overlooked sites. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-12T08:20:22.495Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4r55hvz87r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4r55hvz87r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Matt","last_name":"Newton","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Single Molecule Imaging","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9","Genome Editing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9xkgn447vz","title":"In vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Summary: Despite remarkable clinical efficacies of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that inhibition of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances tumor immunity and ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. Cop1 inhibition stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the TNBC tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-23T12:28:59.397Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xkgn447vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xkgn447vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Collin","last_name":"Tokheim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binbin","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Protein Degradation","Immunotherapy","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mx6yf4mg5k","title":"Data supporting the manuscript","description":"Data support the figures of the manuscript, entitled \"Gut microbiome-derived butyric acid and cinnamic acid attenuate acute lung injury via inhibiting NF-κB pathway in diet-induced obese mice\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-23T15:47:22.442Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mx6yf4mg5k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mx6yf4mg5k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kejie","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut Microbiome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nf3hm5dj9m","title":"ALI data","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:14.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nf3hm5dj9m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nf3hm5dj9m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jingtao","last_name":"LI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Respiratory System"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ss6ghkd8bk","title":"Inhibition of M1 polarization in alveolar macrophages by vasoactive intestinal peptide attenuates LPS-induced acute lung injury in vivo and vitro","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:35.873Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ss6ghkd8bk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ss6ghkd8bk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"honggang","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Injury"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121553,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":139,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121553,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121008,"restricted":61,"embargoed":484},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2083},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":607},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":445},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":401},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":361},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":307},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":243},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":211}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":118974},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":119},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":492},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":401},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":351},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":323},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":289}]}}